WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:04.763
Multi-GPU success depends on the software stack understanding the available devices.

00:00:04.763 --> 00:00:08.578
Drivers, compute runtimes, inference engines, model formats, operating system,

00:00:08.578 --> 00:00:12.393
and libraries must agree on supported hardware and features.

00:00:12.393 --> 00:00:15.360
CUDA is a common NVIDIA compute platform.

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:18.751
ROCm supports selected AMD hardware and operating-system combinations.

00:00:18.751 --> 00:00:22.142
Vulkan can provide cross-platform acceleration in compatible runtimes.

00:00:22.142 --> 00:00:25.533
Capability and maturity vary by device and workload.

00:00:25.533 --> 00:00:28.501
Apple Metal supports acceleration on Apple platforms,

00:00:28.501 --> 00:00:34.011
usually through integrated unified-memory designs rather than a conventional collection of discrete cards.

00:00:34.011 --> 00:00:39.522
Multi-device expectations from desktop GPU systems should not be assumed to transfer directly.

00:00:39.522 --> 00:00:40.172
llama.

00:00:40.172 --> 00:00:46.106
cpp provides multi-GPU concepts such as device selection and model splitting for supported backends.

00:00:46.106 --> 00:00:52.465
Server-oriented runtimes such as vLLM may offer parallel execution and scheduling at a high level,

00:00:52.465 --> 00:00:55.008
with different hardware and configuration requirements.

00:00:55.008 --> 00:00:59.247
Configuration includes visible devices, memory limits, split strategy, cache, precision,

00:00:59.247 --> 00:01:01.790
batch behavior, networking, and service controls.

00:01:01.790 --> 00:01:07.301
A copied command may be wrong for a different topology or runtime version.

00:01:07.301 --> 00:01:11.540
Maintain a known working combination of drivers, runtime, model, configuration,

00:01:11.540 --> 00:01:12.812
and operating system.

00:01:12.812 --> 00:01:18.322
Test upgrades, record versions, preserve rollback, and avoid changing several layers at once.

00:01:18.322 --> 00:01:22.137
Software stability can determine whether expensive hardware is useful.

00:01:22.137 --> 00:01:26.800
Containers can improve repeatability but do not erase host-driver, device, kernel,

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:28.072
and runtime requirements.

00:01:28.072 --> 00:01:32.734
Record both host and container environments, device visibility, mounted models, permissions,

00:01:32.734 --> 00:01:33.780
and launch configuration.
